# TokenFlow team server reverse proxy, Caddy variant.
# See docs/team-server.md for the full guide. Two variants are documented
# below; only ONE site block may be active at a time (Caddy rejects two
# blocks that answer the same address), so variant (b) is commented out.
# Copy the one you want, replace team.example.com with your real name, and
# delete or leave the other commented.
#
# {$TOKENFLOW_UPSTREAM:127.0.0.1:7790} defaults to bare-metal/systemd, where
# Caddy and the team server share the host's loopback. deploy/docker-compose.yml
# sets TOKENFLOW_UPSTREAM=tokenflow-team:7790 on the caddy service, since
# inside that container 127.0.0.1 is caddy's own loopback, not the team
# server's; without the override every proxied request would 502.
#
# Caddy requests a public certificate automatically for a name that
# resolves on the public internet and answers inbound 80/443 from it. A
# name that only resolves on your LAN cannot get one that way: add
# "tls internal" inside the site block to use Caddy's own local CA instead
# (every laptop then needs to trust that CA once), or configure a DNS-01
# challenge module if this host has no public inbound path at all.

# ---- Variant (a): LAN only, everything behind the proxy (ACTIVE) ----------
# Every path goes through the proxy; nothing here is meant to be public.

team.example.com {
	reverse_proxy {$TOKENFLOW_UPSTREAM:127.0.0.1:7790}
	# tls internal
}

# ---- Variant (b): only the GitHub webhook is public (commented out) ------
# Everything except /github/webhook stays on the private network; that one
# path is the single thing GitHub needs to reach, and it carries no bearer
# token of its own (its auth is the webhook secret, an HMAC over the raw
# body; see docs/github-app.md). The match below is by URL path, not by
# where the request came from, so also keep this host's inbound ports
# firewalled to your LAN/VPN except for what the proxy needs to expose.
# Delete variant (a) above before enabling this one; both cannot answer the
# same address.
#
# team.example.com {
# 	@webhook path /github/webhook
# 	handle @webhook {
# 		reverse_proxy {$TOKENFLOW_UPSTREAM:127.0.0.1:7790}
# 	}
# 	handle {
# 		# private_ranges matches RFC1918 / loopback / link-local addresses;
# 		# behind another load balancer in front of Caddy, add
# 		# "trusted_proxies <cidr>" so the real client address is used here
# 		# instead of the proxy's own. Inside Docker with published ports, the
# 		# address Caddy sees is often the bridge gateway (itself a private
# 		# range), which would match every real internet client too; check
# 		# Caddy's access log for the address it actually sees there before
# 		# relying on this matcher in a container. On bare metal this is fine.
# 		@private remote_ip private_ranges
# 		handle @private {
# 			reverse_proxy {$TOKENFLOW_UPSTREAM:127.0.0.1:7790}
# 		}
# 		respond 404
# 	}
# }
